{"language":"FCL-1","records":[{"id":"r1","type":"claim","text":"The pattern of agreeing and later disputing what was agreed indicates the agreements themselves are underspecified — verbal, vague in scope and deadline, with no shared definition of 'done' — rather than that anyone is necessarily acting in bad faith.","scope":"household of three with recurring chore disputes"},{"id":"r2","type":"claim","text":"Because work schedules change, a fixed rota will repeatedly break; what is needed is a cheap, scheduled renegotiation step, not a stricter rota.","grounds":"each schedule change re-opens allocation, and renegotiation-by-exception is exactly the conversation that is failing"},{"id":"r3","type":"claim","text":"The avoidant friend is the highest-risk element: a chore arrangement accepted by two of three people has no legitimacy and will generate quiet resentment."},{"id":"r4","type":"objection","target":["r5"],"text":"A written chore record can read as bureaucratic or accusatory among friends, and if introduced while one person is already withdrawing it can be received as a weapon.","bearing":"the record should be minimal (one shared list), framed as protecting the friendship, and introduced in a calm jointly-agreed moment rather than mid-dispute; its existence does not by itself repair the conversational channel"},{"id":"r5","type":"commitment","text":"Keep one shared, visible record of agreements: task, owner, day, and what counts as done; treat it as the reference when memories diverge, with a pre-agreed rule for disputed items (e.g., disputed tasks rotate by default).","action":"introduce the record only after the participation channel with the avoidant friend is reopened","consequence":"disputes about meaning become disputes about an editable text, and default rules mean divergence does not require adjudication each time","depends":["r1"]},{"id":"r6","type":"commitment","text":"Replace one-off agreements with a brief weekly reset (ten minutes or a group message) keyed to the coming week's schedules, so schedule change becomes the normal input to allocation rather than a breach of it.","consequence":"renegotiation becomes routine and low-stakes","depends":["r2"]},{"id":"r7","type":"commitment","text":"Address the avoidance by lowering the cost of participation before raising pressure: one-on-one rather than two-against-one, asynchronous channels where possible, limited choices rather than open debates, and one direct gentle question about what makes these talks hard.","consequence":"if avoidance persists despite low-cost channels, the problem is reclassified as a friendship-level problem and named as such rather than treated as scheduling","depends":["r3"]},{"id":"r8","type":"commitment","text":"Before proposing any system, seek a meta-agreement: all three concede the current loop is broken and that trying something small is preferable to winning the next dispute.","consequence":"agreement that the loop fails is cheaper to obtain than agreement on any particular rota"},{"id":"r9","type":"problem","text":"Only one side of the dispute is given; it is unresolved whether the asker's own conduct (tone, perceived policing), an underlying fairness grievance about uneven effort, or unrelated friendship strain drives the pattern.","mentions":["r1","r3"]},{"id":"r10","type":"problem","text":"The fairness metric is genuinely open — equal tasks, equal time, or tasks weighted by time and unpleasantness — and shifting schedules may make equal-time unfair; it must be chosen explicitly with the flatmates rather than assumed to be shared.","depends":["r2"]},{"id":"r11","type":"claim","text":"If some reinterpretation of past agreements is strategic — escaping a task one regrets accepting — explicitness still helps by raising the cost of reinterpretation, but it will surface an underlying fairness grievance rather than dissolve it; that grievance then needs discussion, not documentation.","scope":"the case where disputes about meaning are partly motivated"},{"id":"u1","type":"use","text":"Sequence the moves: repair the low-cost participation channel with the avoidant friend (r7), then the meta-agreement that the loop is broken (r8), then a minimal shared record with a dispute rule (r5), then the weekly schedule-keyed reset (r6), keeping r4's caveat in force throughout and settling r10 explicitly with all three flatmates.","depends":["r7","r8","r5","r6","r4","r10"]}],"uptake":[{"record":"u1","note":"main proposed course of action, offered as a conjectured sequence rather than a certified solution"},{"record":"r4","note":"adopted as a standing constraint on how the written record is introduced"},{"record":"r10","note":"to be settled explicitly with the flatmates rather than defaulted"}]}